import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
import java.io.ObjectOutputStream;
import java.lang.reflect.InvocationHandler;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;
import java.lang.reflect.Proxy;
import java.net.Socket;

public class RpcClientProxy {
    private String host;
    private int port;

    public RpcClientProxy(String host, int port) {
        this.host = host;
        this.port = port;
    }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <T> T createProxy(Class<T> interfaceClass) {
        return (T) Proxy.newProxyInstance(interfaceClass.getClassLoader(),
                new Class<?>[]{interfaceClass},
                new RemoteInvocationHandler(interfaceClass));
    }

    private class RemoteInvocationHandler implements InvocationHandler {
        private Class<?> interfaceClass;

        public RemoteInvocationHandler(Class<?> interfaceClass) {
            this.interfaceClass = interfaceClass;
        }

        @Override
        public Object invoke(Object proxy, Method method, Object[] args) throws Throwable {
            Socket socket = new Socket(host, port);
            try (ObjectOutputStream out = new ObjectOutputStream(socket.getOutputStream());
                 ObjectInputStream in = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream())) {
                out.writeUTF(interfaceClass.getName());
                out.writeUTF(method.getName());
                out.writeObject(method.getParameterTypes());
                out.writeObject(args);
                out.flush();

                Object result = in.readObject();
                if (result instanceof Throwable) {
                    throw (Throwable) result;
                }
                return result;
            } finally {
                socket.close();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, we create a `RpcClientProxy` class that takes the server's host and port as parameters. The `createProxy` method dynamically generates a proxy object for the given interface using Java's `Proxy` class. The proxy object is created with an `InvocationHandler` implementation, `RemoteInvocationHandler`, which handles the remote method invocations.

The `RemoteInvocationHandler` serializes the interface name, method name, parameter types, and arguments, sends them over the network to the server, and receives the result. If the result is an exception, it is thrown back to the client. The `Socket` class is used for network communication, and `ObjectInputStream` and `ObjectOutputStream` are used for serialization and deserialization of data.

This solution provides a basic implementation of an RPC client proxy that can be used to remotely invoke methods on a server.

