    // Your code here
}
```

The function should take in the sorted array of integers `nums` and return the unique element that appears only once.

For example, given the input array `nums = [1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 8, 8]`, the function should return `2` as it is the unique element in the array.

```java
public int singleNonDuplicate(int[] nums) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = nums.length - 1;
    while (lo < hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (mid % 2 == 1) {
            mid--;
        }
        if (nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]) {
            lo = mid + 2;
        } else {
            hi = mid;
        }
    }
    return nums[lo];
}
```

The solution uses binary search to find the unique element in the sorted array. It iterates through the array by adjusting the `lo` and `hi` pointers based on the comparison of elements at even indexes. The function returns the unique element found after the binary search.

