import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

interface DataSourceResolver {
    String resolve(String dataSourceId);
}

class DataSourceCompositeResolver implements DataSourceResolver {
    private final Map<String, DataSourceResolver> resolvers;
    private final boolean lenient;

    public DataSourceCompositeResolver(DataSourceResolver[] resolvers, boolean lenient) {
        this.resolvers = new HashMap<>();
        for (DataSourceResolver resolver : resolvers) {
            this.resolvers.put(resolver.getClass().getSimpleName(), resolver);
        }
        this.lenient = lenient;
    }

    @Override
    public String resolve(String dataSourceId) {
        for (DataSourceResolver resolver : resolvers.values()) {
            String resolvedDataSource = resolver.resolve(dataSourceId);
            if (resolvedDataSource != null) {
                return resolvedDataSource;
            }
        }
        return lenient ? "Leniently resolved data source for " + dataSourceId : null;
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `DataSourceCompositeResolver` class implements the `DataSourceResolver` interface and maintains a map of data source resolvers. The `resolve` method iterates through the available resolvers and attempts to resolve the data source using each resolver. If a resolver successfully resolves the data source, the resolved data source is returned. If none of the resolvers can resolve the data source and the resolver is lenient, a message indicating lenient resolution is returned. Otherwise, `null` is returned. This implementation allows the composite resolver to handle missing data sources in a lenient manner.

