import org.jetbrains.annotations.NotNull;
import org.jetbrains.annotations.Nullable;

import java.util.function.Supplier;

public class DependencyManager {
    private CommandHandler commandHandler; // Assume the existence of a CommandHandler class

    public DependencyManager(CommandHandler commandHandler) {
        this.commandHandler = commandHandler;
    }

    public ContextResolver<?> createResolver(CommandParameter parameter) {
        if (!parameter.hasAnnotation(Dependency.class)) {
            return null;
        }

        Supplier<?> value = commandHandler.getDependency(parameter.getType());
        if (value == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unable to resolve dependency for parameter " +
                    parameter.getName() + " in " + parameter.getDeclaringCommand().getPath().toRealString());
        }

        return context -> value.get();
    }
}
```

In the solution, we create a `DependencyManager` class that takes a `CommandHandler` instance in its constructor. The `createResolver` method checks if the given parameter has the `Dependency` annotation. If it does, it obtains the value of the parameter from the command handler's dependencies using the `getDependency` method. If the value is obtained successfully, it creates and returns a context resolver that resolves the parameter by returning the value from the supplier. If the value cannot be obtained, it throws an `IllegalArgumentException` with a descriptive error message.

