import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

public void destroyProcessAndWait(Process process, long gracefulTimeout, TimeUnit gracefulTimeoutUnit, long forcefulTimeout, TimeUnit forcefulTimeoutUnit) {
    try {
        // Attempt graceful destroy and wait for the specified timeout
        if (process != null) {
            process.destroy();
            if (!process.waitFor(gracefulTimeout, gracefulTimeoutUnit)) {
                // Graceful destroy timed out, attempt forceful destroy
                process.destroyForcibly();
                process.waitFor(forcefulTimeout, forcefulTimeoutUnit);
            }
        }
    } catch (InterruptedException e) {
        // Handle interruption if the current thread is interrupted
        Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
    } catch (Exception e) {
        // Handle any other exceptions during process destruction
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `destroyProcessAndWait` method first attempts to gracefully destroy the given process using the `destroy` method. It then waits for the specified graceful timeout using the `waitFor` method. If the graceful destroy operation times out, it proceeds to forcefully destroy the process using the `destroyForcibly` method and waits for the specified forceful timeout. The method handles interruptions and other exceptions that may occur during the process destruction.

