import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class AsyncRateLimiter {
    private final Semaphore semaphore;
    private final ExecutorService executor;

    public AsyncRateLimiter(int permits) {
        this.semaphore = new Semaphore(permits);
        this.executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
    }

    public void acquireAndExecute(int permits, Runnable task) {
        try {
            semaphore.acquire(permits);
            executor.submit(() -> {
                try {
                    task.run();
                } finally {
                    semaphore.release(permits);
                }
            });
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

In the provided solution, the `AsyncRateLimiter` class uses a `Semaphore` to control the acquisition of permits and an `ExecutorService` to execute tasks asynchronously. The `acquireAndExecute` method acquires the specified number of permits using the `Semaphore` and then submits the task to the `ExecutorService` for asynchronous execution. Upon completion of the task, the acquired permits are released back to the `Semaphore`. The `shutdown` method is provided to gracefully shut down the `ExecutorService` when it is no longer needed.

