Your task is to implement a Java program that accomplishes the following:
1. Read the JSON file "images.json" containing image data in the following format:
   ```json
   {
     "images": [
       {
         "title": "Nature",
         "url": "https://example.com/nature.jpg"
       },
       {
         "title": "Cityscape",
         "url": "https://example.com/cityscape.jpg"
       },
       {
         "title": "Wildlife",
         "url": "https://example.com/wildlife.jpg"
       }
     ]
   }
   ```
2. Use the Gson library to parse the JSON data into a collection of Java objects representing the images.
3. Utilize the Picasso library to load and display the images in a graphical user interface.

Your program should be structured in a modular and organized manner, handling potential exceptions and errors gracefully.

```java
import com.google.gson.Gson;
import com.squareup.picasso.Picasso;
import com.squareup.picasso.Target;

import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.io.FileReader;
import java.io.IOException;

public class ImageDisplayApp {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // Read the JSON file and parse the data using Gson
            Gson gson = new Gson();
            ImageData imageData = gson.fromJson(new FileReader("images.json"), ImageData.class);

            // Create a GUI to display the images
            JFrame frame = new JFrame("Image Display");
            frame.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
            frame.setLayout(new FlowLayout());

            // Load and display each image using Picasso
            for (ImageInfo imageInfo : imageData.getImages()) {
                JLabel imageLabel = new JLabel(imageInfo.getTitle());
                frame.add(imageLabel);

                // Use Picasso to load and display the image
                Target target = new JLabelTarget(imageLabel);
                Picasso.get().load(imageInfo.getUrl()).into(target);
            }

            frame.pack();
            frame.setVisible(true);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Error reading the JSON file: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    // Define classes to represent the JSON data
    static class ImageData {
        private ImageInfo[] images;

        public ImageInfo[] getImages() {
            return images;
        }
    }

    static class ImageInfo {
        private String title;
        private String url;

        public String getTitle() {
            return title;
        }

        public String getUrl() {
            return url;
        }
    }

    // Custom Picasso Target to load images into JLabel
    static class JLabelTarget implements Target {
        private final JLabel label;

        JLabelTarget(JLabel label) {
            this.label = label;
        }

        @Override
        public void onBitmapLoaded(Image image, Picasso.LoadedFrom from) {
            label.setIcon(new ImageIcon(image));
        }

        @Override
        public void onBitmapFailed(Exception e, Drawable errorDrawable) {
            label.setText("Failed to load image");
        }

        @Override
        public void onPrepareLoad(Drawable placeHolderDrawable) {
            // Do nothing
        }
    }
}
```

In this solution, we first use the Gson library to parse the JSON data from the "images.json" file into Java objects representing the images. Then, we create a graphical user interface using Swing to display the images. For each image, we use the Picasso library to load and display the image in a JLabel component. The program handles potential IO exceptions when reading the JSON file and gracefully displays an error message if an image fails to load.

