import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class MedianFinder {
    private PriorityQueue<Integer> maxQueue; // Max-heap to store the lower half of the numbers
    private PriorityQueue<Integer> minQueue; // Min-heap to store the higher half of the numbers

    public MedianFinder() {
        maxQueue = new PriorityQueue<>((o1, o2) -> o2 - o1); // Initialize max-heap with a comparator for reverse order
        minQueue = new PriorityQueue<>(); // Initialize min-heap
    }

    public void addNum(int num) {
        maxQueue.add(num); // Add the number to the max-heap

        minQueue.add(maxQueue.poll()); // Move the maximum element from max-heap to min-heap
        if (minQueue.size() > maxQueue.size()) {
            maxQueue.add(minQueue.poll()); // Rebalance the heaps if necessary
        }
    }

    public double findMedian() {
        if (maxQueue.size() == minQueue.size()) {
            return (double) (maxQueue.peek() + minQueue.peek()) / 2; // If the sizes are equal, return the average of the top elements
        } else {
            return maxQueue.peek(); // Otherwise, return the top element of the max-heap
        }
    }
}
```

The `MedianFinder` class uses two priority queues to maintain the lower and higher halves of the data stream. The `addNum` method adds the number to the max-heap, then rebalances the heaps if necessary. The `findMedian` method returns the median based on the sizes of the heaps. This implementation ensures O(log n) time complexity for adding numbers and O(1) time complexity for finding the median.

