import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class TopoMetaLRUCache<K, V> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final LinkedHashMap<K, V> cache;

    public TopoMetaLRUCache(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.cache = new LinkedHashMap<>(capacity, 0.75f, true) {
            @Override
            protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K, V> eldest) {
                return size() > capacity;
            }
        };
    }

    public synchronized void addMetadata(K key, V metadata) {
        cache.put(key, metadata);
    }

    public synchronized V getMetadata(K key) {
        return cache.get(key);
    }
}
```

In the solution, we define the `TopoMetaLRUCache` class as a generic class that takes key and value types. The constructor initializes the cache with the specified capacity using a `LinkedHashMap` with access order set to true for LRU behavior.

The `addMetadata` method adds metadata to the cache, and the `getMetadata` method retrieves metadata based on the provided key. The `LinkedHashMap` automatically handles LRU eviction when the cache reaches its capacity, as specified in the constructor.

The solution ensures thread safety by using synchronized methods for adding and retrieving metadata. This implementation provides efficient storage and retrieval of topological metadata while maintaining LRU eviction behavior.

