```java
public class Solution {
    public static boolean validateProbabilities(int topicCount, int termCount, Result result) {
        for (int i = 0; i < topicCount; i++) {
            if (result.topicTermProbabilities[i].length != termCount) {
                return false; // Number of terms does not match the expected count
            }
            for (int j = 0; j < termCount; j++) {
                if (!isValidProbability(result.topicTermProbabilities[i][j])) {
                    return false; // Invalid probability found
                }
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < result.documentTopicProbabilities.length; i++) {
            if (result.documentTopicProbabilities[i].length != topicCount) {
                return false; // Number of topics does not match the expected count
            }
            for (int j = 0; j < topicCount; j++) {
                if (!isValidProbability(result.documentTopicProbabilities[i][j])) {
                    return false; // Invalid probability found
                }
            }
        }

        return true; // All probabilities are valid
    }

    private static boolean isValidProbability(double probability) {
        return probability >= 0 && probability <= 1;
    }
}
```

The `validateProbabilities` function iterates through the topic-term and document-topic probabilities, checking their lengths and individual values against the expected counts and validity criteria. The `isValidProbability` function checks whether a given value is a valid probability. If any inconsistency or invalid probability is found, the function returns `false`; otherwise, it returns `true` indicating that all probabilities are valid.

