You are given the definition of a binary tree node and a method `isSameTree` that takes in two binary trees `p` and `q`. The method should return `true` if the two trees are structurally identical and their nodes have the same value, and `false` otherwise. A binary tree is structurally identical if it has the same structure and the nodes have the same value at each position.

The definition of a binary tree node is as follows:
```java
class TreeNode {
    int val;
    TreeNode left;
    TreeNode right;
    TreeNode(int x) { val = x; }
}
```

You need to implement the `isSameTree` method to solve this problem.

```java
public class Solution100 {
    public boolean isSameTree(TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
        if (p == null && q == null) {
            return true;
        }
        if (p == null || q == null) {
            return false;
        }
        if (p.val != q.val) {
            return false;
        }
        return isSameTree(p.left, q.left) && isSameTree(p.right, q.right);
    }
}
```
The `isSameTree` method first checks if both `p` and `q` are `null`, in which case it returns `true` as they are structurally identical. If only one of `p` or `q` is `null`, it returns `false` as they are not structurally identical. Then, it compares the values of the current nodes and recursively checks the left and right subtrees for structural identity. If any of these checks fail, it returns `false`. If all checks pass, it returns `true`, indicating that the trees are structurally identical.

