```java
public class AllField extends Field {

    public static final FieldType FIELD_TYPE = new FieldType();

    static {
        FIELD_TYPE.setIndexOptions(IndexOptions.DOCS_AND_FREQS_AND_POSITIONS);
        FIELD_TYPE.setTokenized(true);
    }

    public static int calculateTermFrequency(String documentContent, String term) {
        Tokenizer tokenizer = new StandardTokenizer();
        tokenizer.setReader(new StringReader(documentContent));
        TokenStream tokenStream = new StopFilter(new LowerCaseFilter(tokenizer), StopAnalyzer.ENGLISH_STOP_WORDS_SET);

        try {
            tokenStream.reset();
            int termFrequency = 0;
            CharTermAttribute charTermAttribute = tokenStream.addAttribute(CharTermAttribute.class);

            while (tokenStream.incrementToken()) {
                String token = charTermAttribute.toString();
                if (token.equals(term)) {
                    termFrequency++;
                }
            }

            tokenStream.end();
            tokenStream.close();

            return termFrequency;
        } catch (IOException e) {
            // Handle exception
            return -1;
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `calculateTermFrequency` method uses Lucene's tokenization and filtering mechanisms to calculate the term frequency of a given word in the document content. It initializes a tokenizer with the document content, applies lowercasing and stop word filtering, and then iterates through the tokens to count the occurrences of the given term. The method returns the calculated term frequency or -1 in case of an exception.

