import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class DocumentParser {
    public LocalDateTime getTransactionTime(final Document parsedDocument) {
        String content = parsedDocument.getContent();
        // Define the pattern for identifying the transaction time format within the content
        Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile("Transaction Time: (\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2} \\d{2}:\\d{2}:\\d{2})");
        Matcher matcher = pattern.matcher(content);

        if (matcher.find()) {
            // Extract the matched transaction time and parse it to LocalDateTime
            String transactionTimeStr = matcher.group(1);
            return LocalDateTime.parse(transactionTimeStr, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"));
        } else {
            // If transaction time is not found or cannot be parsed, return null
            return null;
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `getTransactionTime` method uses regular expressions to define a pattern for identifying the transaction time format within the `content` of the `Document`. It then uses a `Matcher` to find and extract the transaction time information. If the transaction time is found, it is parsed to a `LocalDateTime` object and returned. If the transaction time cannot be found or parsed, the method returns `null`.

