import java.util.Collection;
import java.util.Iterator;

public class CustomIterator<T> implements Iterator<T> {
    private Collection<T> items;
    private Iterator<T> iterator;
    private Config config;

    public CustomIterator(Collection<T> items, Config config) {
        this.items = items;
        this.iterator = items.iterator();
        this.config = config;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean hasNext() {
        return iterator.hasNext();
    }

    @Override
    public T next() {
        return iterator.next();
    }

    @Override
    public void remove() {
        iterator.remove();
    }

    @Override
    public void remove() {
        iterator.remove();
    }

    @Override
    public T next() {
        while (iterator.hasNext()) {
            T next = iterator.next();
            if (!config.specDrops()) {
                continue;
            }
            return next;
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

In this solution, the `CustomIterator` class implements the `Iterator` interface and uses a `Config` object to determine whether to skip over elements during iteration. The `next()` method is modified to skip over elements based on the condition specified by the `specDrops()` method of the `Config` object. If `specDrops()` returns false, the iterator continues to the next element. If `specDrops()` returns true, the iterator proceeds as normal. The `remove()` method is also implemented to remove elements from the underlying collection.

