To optimize the performance of populating the HashMap, we can utilize parallel processing to distribute the workload across multiple threads. This approach can significantly reduce the time taken to populate the HashMap with a large number of entries.

Here's a sample solution using Java's parallel streams to populate the HashMap in parallel:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.stream.LongStream;

public class MapTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long now = System.currentTimeMillis();

        ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

        long numEntries = 1000000000;
        int parallelism = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors(); // Get the number of available processors

        LongStream.range(0, numEntries)
                .parallel() // Enable parallel processing
                .forEach(i -> map.put("topic" + i, 100000));

        System.out.println(System.currentTimeMillis() - now);
    }
}
```

In this solution, we use `ConcurrentHashMap` instead of `HashMap` to ensure thread safety during parallel population. We leverage Java's parallel streams by using `LongStream.range(0, numEntries).parallel().forEach(...)` to distribute the population task across available processor cores.

By utilizing parallel processing, the time taken to populate the HashMap with a large number of entries can be significantly reduced, leading to improved performance.

